# -*- coding: utf-8 -*-
"""Strict Positioning — English reading."""

TITLE = 'Chapter Four — Strict Positioning'
HEAD = '''Twenty-one passages, the most of any chapter and the shortest each — one of them runs to seven Chinese characters, another to ten. The subject is the moment of contact: position, drum-beat, dress, exhaustion, fear. The second passage is the most practical thing in the book, and it is entirely about how to handle men who are afraid — close the ranks, have them sit, have them kneel, have them lie down, have them advance on their knees — ending: <i>if the fear is too great, do not execute anyone; show them a kind face, tell them there is a way to live, and go along the line looking into each man’s duty</i>. Several other passages are the clearest-headed in the book. “The alert of a whole army lasts no more than three days, the watch of a company no more than half a day, the restraint of one man no more than a single rest” — tension has a limit. “Once firm, do not add; when you press forward, do not use everything up; to use everything up is dangerous.” “The multitude does not think itself many, or it has not got the Way.” The title is the first three words of the chapter.'''

PARA = {
 'p4-1': 'The way of war: let the positions be strict; let the government be awe-inspiring; let strength have slack in it; let the breath be at ease; let the mind be one. (“Slack” here is room left over — strength not used to the full. The chapter takes its title from the first three words. Five wants, and they set the key of everything that follows.)',
 'p4-2': 'The way of war: grade duty by rightness; set up the squads; fix the ranks; make the lines true across and along; examine name against reality. Standing, they advance stooping; sitting, they advance on the knee. If they are afraid, close the ranks; if the position is dangerous, have them sit. Those far off, if they can see, are not afraid; those near, if they do not look about, do not scatter. Lower the position, lower the flanks, have the armoured men sit, swear them in and walk them forward slowly. When the position has reached the foot and the armour, reckon it by light and heavy; startle the horses and let foot and armour raise a shout; if they are afraid, close the ranks again. If they kneel and sit and then lie down, have them advance on their knees while you swear them in gently. If they get up shouting and the drum takes them forward, stop them with the hand-bell. With the gag in the mouth they are sworn in and eat their dry rations, and advance seated, on their knees. Carry out the executions and forbid them to look behind; go before them shouting. But if the fear is too great, do not execute: show them a kind face, tell them there is a way to live, and go along the line looking into each man’s duty. (The base text reads “manage the gag,” which is not Chinese; both witnesses have “hold the gag in the mouth” — the gag is a short stick held crosswise between the teeth to stop talking — and it is emended here. The bell is the large clapper-bell that cancels the drum. The whole passage is about bringing a frightened body of men lower and lower — close, sit, kneel, lie, crawl — and then the last clause takes the whole thing back: if the fear is too great, killing will not help.)',
 'p4-3': 'The men of the three armies are on the alert for half a day; the prohibitions on them never stop; they may not be sent off in shifts to eat. While they are still uncertain, they can be taught and they can be made to submit. (To set the rules while men’s minds are unsettled — the most usable sentence in the book on training.)',
 'p4-4': 'In war: strength makes you last, spirit makes you win; firmness makes you last, taking risks makes you win. The original mind is what makes you firm, fresh spirit is what makes you win; armour is for lasting, weapons are for winning. (Three pairs of “last” and “win” split defence and offence into two kinds of capital: strength, firmness, armour and the original mind on one side; spirit, risk, weapons and fresh spirit on the other.)',
 'p4-5': 'Chariots are made firm by close order, foot by sitting, armour by weight; weapons win by lightness. When a man has the mind to win, he sees nothing but the enemy; when a man has the mind to fear, he sees nothing but what he fears. The two minds settle by turns; the two advantages come to the same thing; each of the two has its office, and it is for judgment alone to see which. (The Siku text reads “an <i>army</i> is made firm by close order.” “He sees nothing but the enemy,” “he sees nothing but what he fears” are the two truest sentences in the book about soldiers: the winning mind and the fearing mind are not two kinds of man but one man at two hours of the day.)',
 'p4-6': 'In war: to use the light for light work is dangerous; to use the heavy for heavy work gets nothing done; to use the light for heavy work is defeat; to use the heavy for light work is how one fights. So in war light and heavy serve one another. In camp be careful of armour and weapons, on the march of the formation and the ranks, in battle of advance and halt. (The Siku text has, in place of “on the march of the formation and the ranks,” a shorter “be careful of the ranks.” Three cares, one for each of camp, march and battle.)',
 'p4-7': 'In war: be careful and you are content; lead from the front and they submit. If those above fuss, the men are light; if those above are unhurried, the men are weighty. Beat the drum urgently and they are light; beat it slowly and they are weighty. Dress close and plain and they are light; dress fine and they are weighty. (Three pairs of light and heavy: one in the mind, one in the drum-beat, one in the clothes — inside and outside, all of them adjustable.)',
 'p4-8': 'When horses and chariots are sound and armour and weapons keen, even the light becomes heavy. If those above merely fall in with the rest, nothing is gained; if those above are wholly high-handed, many die; if those above cling to life, doubt is everywhere below; if those above are bent on dying, still they do not win. (Four faults of a commander. The first and the last deserve attention: clinging to life will not do, and neither will being set on death.)',
 'p4-9': 'Men die for five reasons: they die for love, they die for anger, they die for awe, they die for rightness, they die for gain. (Five words, five states of mind, and not one word more.)',
 'p4-10': 'The way of war: training binds men so that they hold death light; the Way binds men so that they die in the right. (Training and the Way side by side: one makes a man unafraid to die, the other gives his death a name.)',
 'p4-11': 'In war: it may be victory or not, it may be from heaven or from man. (Ten characters — the shortest passage in the book. The outcome in the first clause, the cause in the second, and no verdict on either. This book never guarantees a result.)',
 'p4-12': 'In war: the alert of the three armies lasts no more than three days; the watch of one company no more than half a day; the restraint of one man no more than a single rest. (The Siku text reads “no more than an instant.” Three armies, one company, one man, and the span shortens at each step — tension has a limit. The clearest-headed sentence in the book about what men can bear.)',
 'p4-13': 'The greatest use the root; next after them use the branch. Grasp the outline and hold to the fine detail; whether root or branch, it is judgment that decides — and this is war. (The root is benevolence and instruction, the branch is stratagem, following from the first chapter. Both ends of the book are settled in one line.)',
 'p4-14': 'As to victory: the three armies win by being one man. (Seven characters. The same thing as “the general is the body, the soldiers are the limbs” in chapter three, said another way.)',
 'p4-15': 'As to the drum: there is a drum for the flags, a drum for the chariots, a drum for the horses, a drum for the foot, a drum for the weapons, a drum for the head, a drum for the feet, and a drum for all together. (Seven drums are in fact listed — flags, chariots, horses, foot, weapons, head, feet — and the Siku text reads “the <i>seven</i> drums all together,” one character that makes the sentence plain. This page emends only where both witnesses agree, so the base reading stands and the case is recorded. A “drum for the head” and a “drum for the feet” are drum-signals for raising the head and lifting the foot; each has its beat.)',
 'p4-16': 'In war: once firm, do not add; when you press forward, do not use everything up — to use everything up is always dangerous. (Three clauses saying one thing: leave a margin. It answers the first passage of the chapter, “let strength have slack in it.”)',
 'p4-17': 'In war: the hard thing is not the formation but getting men to the point where they can be formed; the hard thing is not getting them to the point where they can be formed but getting them to the point where they can be used; the hard thing is not knowing but doing. Men in each place have their own nature, and nature differs from region to region; teaching makes custom, and custom differs from region to region; it takes the Way to transform custom. (Three steps, and then, unexpectedly, local character — the only place in the book that speaks of regional difference, and it puts the Way above custom.)',
 'p4-18': 'Whether many or few: having won, act as though you had not. The weapons do not proclaim their keenness, the armour its strength, the chariots their soundness, the horses their quality; and the multitude does not think itself many — or it has not got the Way. (The Sibu congkan scribe miswrites the word for “or not”; the Siku text has a different opening. Five “do not proclaim” in a row make the finest parallel in the book: having won, nothing may be boasted of.)',
 'p4-19': 'In war: if you win, share the credit with the multitude; if you are going to fight again, make rewards and punishments heavier; if you are beaten, take the fault on yourself; and if you fight again, swear them in and go in front, and do not use the earlier method again. Win or lose, do not go back on it — this is called the right rule. (The Siku text reads “swear <i>himself</i> and go in front,” and has “win or lose” with a different second graph; the Sibu congkan scribe writes a near-identical graph for “oneself.” “Do not use the earlier method again” is Sunzi’s “a victory is not repeated.”)',
 'p4-20': 'With the people: rescue them by benevolence, make them fight by rightness, decide by wisdom, close with the enemy by courage, make them single-minded by good faith, urge them on by gain, bring them through by achievement. So let the mind hit the mark of benevolence and conduct the mark of rightness. To be equal to affairs is wisdom; to be equal to great affairs is courage; to be equal to long affairs is good faith. Yield, and there is concord; the men settle among themselves. Treat them as your own children and do not stand on precedent; let them vie in worth and be willing to act for others, and the men are glad at heart and give their strength. (The last four clauses have never been agreed on, and the witnesses differ over them as well: the Sibu congkan text has a different graph in “the men settle among themselves,” and the Siku text in “treat them as your own children.” This page does not emend, and the translation follows the usual reading.)',
 'p4-21': 'In war: strike what is weak and still, avoid what is strong and still; strike what is worn and tired, avoid what is at ease and unspent; strike what is in great fear, avoid what is only a little wary. Such was the government of antiquity. (The Siku text has a variant for “at ease and unspent.” Each of the three pairs turns on a single word — still, tired, afraid — and the same word, at a different degree, reverses the order. “Such was the government of antiquity” is this book’s way of closing a chapter, and it comes four times in the five.)',
}
